# CVE-2026-46600: golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage (x/net before 0.56.0).
# Newly disclosed sibling of the -46595/-46597 batch above; surfaced by a
# Trivy DB update on 2026-08-12.
# phase-820 CORRECTION: the previous note claimed "the same four Go-based
# monitoring images" and named haproxy-exporter (now deleted — see the Phase
# 810/820 block below). A no-ignorefile scan of every pinned image on
# 2026-08-14 shows the real affected set is wider. Re-measured 2026-08-19
# (issue #451) against the deployed set after this PR's bumps: cadvisor
# v0.52.1, alloy v1.18.1, grafana 13.0.6-ubuntu, loki 3.7.6, node-exporter
# v1.12.1 and redis_exporter v1.87.0. The prometheus v3.14.0 and alertmanager
# v0.34.0 bumps clear it from both; promtail is no longer deployed (replaced
# by alloy, phase-825).
# Why no fix (third-party): pinned tags that compile x/net transitively;
# fixing requires an upstream rebuild, and none has shipped.
# Carriers (generated 2026-08-19):
#   gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor:v0.52.1, grafana/alloy:v1.18.1,
#   grafana/grafana:13.0.6-ubuntu, grafana/loki:3.7.6,
#   oliver006/redis_exporter:v1.87.0, prom/node-exporter:v1.12.1
# Each is a pinned third-party image we cannot rebuild, so the fix
# only reaches us when the publisher ships a new tag.
# ⚠ NOT TRUE OF ja4proxy:2.0.0 — that is a Go stdlib finding
# (deploy/docker/Dockerfile.go-proxy pins golang:1.26.5-alpine) and Go 1.26.6
# fixes it. This waiver must NOT be read as covering our own image; the bump
# is tracked as its own phase, together with splitting .trivyignore into
# first-party and third-party files so a sidecar waiver can never again
# silently suppress a finding in the product.
# Why not exploitable (third-party only): dns/dnsmessage parses
# host-originated DNS records, which none of those components reach in our
# deployment. Re-review at expiry.
CVE-2026-46600 exp:2026-09-12


# ── Go 1.26.5 stdlib batch, surfaced by a Trivy DB update on 2026-08-15 ──
# Six stdlib CVEs (encoding/asn1 DoS and siblings), all present in
# `stdlib v1.26.5`.
# Carrier set re-measured 2026-08-19 (issue #451). The previous text named
# prometheus:v3.13.2 and alertmanager:v0.33.1; the bumps in this PR
# (v3.14.0 / v0.34.0) CLEAR all six from both images. The real remaining set
# is cadvisor v0.52.1, alloy v1.18.1, grafana 13.0.6-ubuntu, loki 3.7.6,
# node-exporter v1.12.1 and redis_exporter v1.87.0.
# Why no fix HERE: all six are fixed in Go 1.26.6, but these are pinned
# upstream tags we cannot rebuild — the fix only reaches us when each project
# publishes an image built on 1.26.6. Registry re-checked 2026-08-19: every one
# of the six is already on its newest published tag.
# Carriers (generated 2026-08-19):
#   gcr.io/cadvisor/cadvisor:v0.52.1, grafana/alloy:v1.18.1,
#   grafana/grafana:13.0.6-ubuntu, grafana/loki:3.7.6,
#   oliver006/redis_exporter:v1.87.0, prom/node-exporter:v1.12.1
# Each is a pinned third-party image we cannot rebuild, so the fix
# only reaches us when the publisher ships a new tag.
# ⚠ NOT waived for our own images. The identical CVEs were present in
# ja4proxy:2.0.0 and Phase 822 FIXED them by bumping the builder to
# golang:1.26.6-alpine; that image now scans clean with no ignorefile at all.
# This is exactly the split this file exists to enforce — see the header.
# Why not exploitable here: these are DoS/parsing bugs in asn1 and related
# stdlib paths. All four components are internal-only scrape targets on the
# monitoring network with no host port exposure; none parses attacker-supplied
# ASN.1 or terminates TLS from an untrusted peer.
# Re-review at expiry: check for upstream rebuilds on Go 1.26.6.
CVE-2026-33818 exp:2026-09-12
